    "researchBrief": "The story of human hair being used as a protective barrier in oceans has been corroborated by multiple sources. The project, led by NGOs Orla Sem Lixo Transforma (OSLT) and Fiotrar, with support from the Boticário Group Foundation for Nature Conservation, has been implemented in Guanabara Bay, Rio de Janeiro. The barriers, made from donated human hair, are designed to absorb oil and other pollutants, with studies showing that one gram of hair can absorb an average of five grams of oil. The initiative has undergone a series of tests over the past year to adapt the technology to the specific environmental conditions of the bay. The project not only helps in cleaning up pollution but also repurposes what would otherwise be waste, making it a sustainable and low-cost solution. The barriers are attached to existing floating rubbish containment booms and can remain in the water for up to four months, capturing oil, biodiesel, and other contaminants. The project has received support from local salons, which donate hair clippings that would otherwise be discarded. The initiative is part of a broader effort to protect the mangrove forests in the area, which are crucial for carbon sequestration and maintaining biodiversity. The project has been featured in multiple news outlets, including Agência Brasil, Africanews, European Pulse, and TV BRICS, all of which provide consistent details about the initiative."
